WEBVTT

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:01.700
This is the dish we're going to do tonight.

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:07.220
I call it a pork rougout with onions and German noodles, known as spetzel.

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:10.080
It's a very interesting dish and a very easy dish to do.

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:16.300
You braise the pork and chicken stock with a little red tomato paste

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.840
and thicken the sauce and spread it over the dish.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:20.840
Very, very interesting.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:24.200
We're also tonight continuing our interview with Mr. Robert Levy.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:26.100
He's a restaurant critic of the Boston Globe,

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.680
and I think you'll find him an extremely interesting man to listen to.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:59.760
Thank you. Welcome to Edge Place. Tonight we're going to do a pork dish. Again, it's another one of them simple dishes. We're going to use a pork tenderloin.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:08.600
some onions, we braise the pork in some chicken stock, thicken it with a little roux, add some

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:14.460
tomato paste, and serve it with a German noodles called German Spätzle. I'll also show you

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:18.550
how to make that, that's a very simple dish to make. We'll start out by cutting this pork

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:25.560
tenderloin. You can use a pork loin boned out, you could use pork chops, actually if

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:28.360
the bone was on it, it wouldn't make much difference, that would be a personal choice.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:29.970
I happen to like.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.790
bones but a lot of people don't. It's a good idea to heat the pan up if you're

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:41.520
going to do any cooking. Heat the pan, put the oil in it and I'm using olive oil

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.600
tonight because it just seems to go good with this dish. You could use cooking

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:53.590
oil or you could use just about anything you want. The pan is getting warm. I'm

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:59.980
going to add a little oil now. A little or as much as you can.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.960
you choose. While the oil is heating up, I'm going to flour this pork now. This is just

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:11.220
a mixture of plain flour and salt and pepper. By the way, this is all the salt I'll use

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:15.830
in the dishes. It's already in this flour, so I'm not much on using salt as we've talked

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:23.060
about this before. The reason we flour this, it helps the meat brown better. Also, when

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:26.700
you're making a dish like this, which is simple to a stew or a ragout or whatever you

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:29.970
want to call it, even though this flour browns on here, it also...

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:38.010
acts as a thickening agent, helps to thicken the meat. You could do this with

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:45.300
lamb, of course you could do it with beef. If you were to do it with lamb, you'd

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:50.730
use probably, instead of using a spatula, I'd use like a pasta, like as an

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:55.370
Italian pasta that looks like rice. They call it orzo, I believe. Use that,

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:57.910
cook that with a few carrot shavings to give it some color.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:05.790
If you're going to use beef, you could just serve it with regular noodles, egg noodles,

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:10.980
or if you choose to, you could use, you know, you could serve it with potatoes for all that matters.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.300
It's an easy dish, but it's a very flavorful dish,

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:18.900
and of course it depends on the fact that you're going to use stock as your cooking liquid.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.300
And again, I'm a firm believer in homemade.

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:23.800
This is all homemade stock here.

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:28.470
If you use canned stock, you want to be careful about the salt content,

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:29.980
because the dish will be very...

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.370
salty and you don't want that. They claim they have a low sodium stock out on the

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:45.140
market but every time I go in the supermarket I never see it so but if you

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:46.420
can find it you'd be better off.

00:04:02.170 --> 00:04:04.750
When you're browning meat, you don't want to crowd the pan too much.

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:08.280
That's about a maximum amount you'd want to get in a pan like that

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:10.990
because if you're crowded too much, the meat doesn't brown.

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:13.990
It stews more than anything else.

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:17.020
You don't get that brown crust on it that you'd like.

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:31.210
This is a winter dish.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:34.660
You'd want to cook, you know, it's not a summer dish,

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:36.740
especially if you're cooking pork.

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:42.840
But it's by thickening, I like to thicken the stock a little,

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:47.770
the braising stock, and it seems to adhere to the meat better

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:49.650
and adheres to the noodles better.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:50.900
It gives you a nice flavor.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:57.030
If you choose not to thicken it, you can.

00:04:58.360 --> 00:04:59.980
Your juices will.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.900
be a lot thinner. The flavor will be there, they'll just be a little thinner. It's going

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:08.320
to take a minute or two for that stuff to brown. So while that's browning, I'm going

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:15.200
to make the base for the spatula. Again, it's very simple. Basically what you want is one

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:21.490
egg, a cup of flour, that is one egg per cup of flour. You could use as many eggs as much

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:26.280
flour as you want if you've got the conditions to mix it together, and milk. Some people use water,

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:28.680
I like to use milk and nutmeg.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.700
So what I got in here was a liberal two cups of flour.

00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:37.940
Throw in some nutmeg, maybe a quarter teaspoon of nutmeg.

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:41.510
And then just break two eggs right in the middle of it.

00:05:49.570 --> 00:05:51.290
Meanwhile, watch your meat. Don't let it burn.

00:05:58.820 --> 00:05:59.540
Mix this together.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.320
It'll get stiff very, very fast, but don't worry about that.

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:08.230
You're going to loosen it all up with some milk or any kind of liquid if you're going to use water.

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:19.180
This is a basic, if you've ever made pasta, pasta is nothing but flour and eggs,

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:23.580
and some people put a little oil in it, some people don't put any water in it.

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:24.480
It depends.

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:29.510
But this is a German noodle mixture, but it's a basic pasta mix, except you're using milk.

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:34.160
And you're going to make this a little looser than you would normally make pasta.

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:42.800
This is somewhere between a bread dough and a pancake batter.

00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:59.960
I hit it right on the nose with this, although I might put a little more milk in there.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:17.440
just a dab more. That's all there is to it. Now I got a special tool for making spatial. You'll

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:23.990
see what it is in a minute. But what a lot of people do is they just take a metal pie plate

00:07:23.990 --> 00:07:29.150
and they poke some quarter inch holes in it and put

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:35.540
the special dough in it and scrape it through the holes. And it works fine because I did

00:07:35.540 --> 00:07:57.980
that before I bought this special maker. You can buy them in kitchen appliance stores. I

00:07:57.980 --> 00:07:59.880
generally do when I'm going to make something like this.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:01.340
I pre-cooked the vegetables.

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:03.480
Sometimes I cook carrots with this.

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:05.280
Other times it's onions.

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:06.520
I like onions with pork.

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:09.740
They seem to, onions and pork seem to go together very well.

00:08:10.420 --> 00:08:13.020
What I did is I just took some white cooking onions.

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:15.880
Like you normally cook around Christmas time.

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:17.740
A lot of people have them Christmas and at Thanksgiving.

00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:19.140
And blanch them.

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:24.380
And when I make the sauce, probably 10 minutes before the dish is done,

00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:26.800
I'll just add these pre-cooked onions so they'll warm through.

00:08:27.250 --> 00:08:29.890
You would do the same thing with carrot if you were adding carrot.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:36.300
to it, as opposed to cooking the vegetables in, in the dish. But then again, I suppose

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:39.420
if you wanted to cook them in there, there would be no reason why you couldn't, but I,

00:08:39.790 --> 00:08:51.090
I find it works better this way. It gives me a little more control over the dish. This

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:58.480
is a special maker here, and what you do is you put the, the dough, or the paste, or whatever

00:08:58.480 --> 00:08:59.800
what you want down in this little

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:04.320
here and this sits over the top of the pot you just run it back and forth like

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:09.450
that and it falls through these holes in the boiling water cooks immediately as

00:09:09.820 --> 00:09:13.180
it falls in it falls to the bottom of the pan and within 30 seconds it rises

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:18.000
right to the top when it rises to the top of the pan it's done now if you

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:23.550
weren't serving it with a sauce of some kind what we do at home and a lot of

00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:28.580
people do is you make spatial like I'm going to show you how it's made drain it

00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:29.890
well then take

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:34.900
some butter and put it in a pan and make noisot butter, noisot butter or whatever the term

00:09:34.900 --> 00:09:39.640
you choose, what it is is nothing but brown butter. You don't want it black now, you want

00:09:39.640 --> 00:09:45.680
it nut brown and then toss the spatula in the nut brown butter and it's absolutely delicious

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:50.820
and it makes a greater compliment if you're, you know, if you're getting a little tired

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:57.770
of potatoes then make some spatula and I think you'll enjoy it very much. The Germans

00:09:57.770 --> 00:09:59.570
use it a lot, the Austrians use it.

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:08.380
A lot of times they'll serve it with weiner schnitzel in Austria,

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:11.930
although usually weiner schnitzel is served with a salad,

00:10:12.050 --> 00:10:13.670
but sometimes they'll serve it with spatials.

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:16.300
This pork is browning up good.

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:19.580
As I said, you could use any kind of pork you wanted.

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:23.670
If you were to take the pork chops and cut the heart out of them,

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:26.990
cut the meat into pieces and saute it,

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:29.610
saute the bones with it and cook the bones in the stew.

00:10:30.250 --> 00:10:32.250
or the ragawis, whatever you want to call it.

00:10:33.400 --> 00:10:36.320
And just before you serve it, take the bones out and put them on my plate,

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:39.500
because I like to chew on the bones anyway, but I'm sure other people do.

00:10:40.170 --> 00:10:42.180
But tenderloins are nice.

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:44.960
What's good about a tenderloin is it's very lean.

00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:46.720
There isn't a lot of fat in the tenderloin.

00:10:46.820 --> 00:10:49.600
Even though it's pork, believe it or not, it's rather lean meat.

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:56.580
So a loin, a boned-out pork loin would be, you know, rather lean meat.

00:10:58.950 --> 00:10:59.970
Pork is very rich.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:05.010
Well, you could get, you know, you could feed a lot of people with it.

00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:11.130
One of the little secrets about meat is the smaller you cut the pieces, the more people you can feed with the same amount.

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:12.970
The bigger the pieces, the less people you can feed.

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:17.820
My mother could feed 20 people with a pound, you know, if she chose to.

00:11:19.010 --> 00:11:28.480
As you probably noticed, this is also a messy way of cooking.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:43.300
We're cooking, we're not cleaning, pretty near done here, pretty near done to the point

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:43.960
of browning.

00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:56.520
Turn the water up so that the water is boiling when we go to make the spatials.

00:12:08.732 --> 00:12:10.432
A cold day just like this is.

00:12:11.552 --> 00:12:12.192
you know really

00:12:12.828 --> 00:12:13.708
It's really delicious.

00:12:17.820 --> 00:12:21.660
Lacoste's Bakery in Medford makes a whole wheat Italian bread.

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:22.940
I don't know if you've ever had it.

00:12:23.484 --> 00:12:25.904
But it's worth making a trip just to get on here and buy it.

00:12:25.944 --> 00:12:27.564
They have it on Monday, Wednesdays, and Fridays.

00:12:28.124 --> 00:12:28.384
Again.

00:12:28.892 --> 00:12:29.952
You get some of that whole wheat.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:35.340
Italian bread and this dish here with the spacial and the two people in the

00:12:35.340 --> 00:12:38.840
world eating better than you that given night. All right I'm gonna take this meat

00:12:38.840 --> 00:12:41.600
out now and I'm gonna put it on the paper towels just to drain it a little

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:44.930
and I'll dump the fat out of the pan and to glaze the pan.

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:48.130
Man, what a little white wine that is.

00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:05.470
You might want to brown this a little more yourself.

00:13:06.520 --> 00:13:08.080
I might brown it a little more too,

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:11.640
but we're kind of restricted with time.

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:13.100
But that's good and brown.

00:13:13.260 --> 00:13:14.720
There's nothing wrong terribly with that dish,

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:17.670
but it could have been a tiny bit browner.

00:13:18.360 --> 00:13:26.360
You just want to get the majority of the fat out.

00:13:26.460 --> 00:13:27.440
You're going to leave some in there.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:38.220
add some white wine to this. If you prefer not to use an alcohol, you can use water.

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:44.580
Water will do exactly the same thing. You want to scrape up the brown goodies on the

00:13:44.580 --> 00:13:44.800
the bottom.

00:13:45.000 --> 00:13:49.860
You can see the liquid in the pan turning a light brown.

00:13:49.980 --> 00:13:52.000
That's getting all the goodies off the bottom of the pan.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:14.810
put the meat back in now. I'd say this would serve four people.

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:20.140
rather generously without any problem at all.

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:28.440
About a pound, a pound and a quarter of meat there, but it's all meat.

00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:29.960
There's no bone or fat, so you get...

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:36.180
a lot out of it. All right, this is some of my famous homemade stock. This is chicken

00:14:36.180 --> 00:14:42.750
stock. Now, you don't want to submerge the meat, but you want to get a generous amount

00:14:42.750 --> 00:14:44.970
in there, maybe halfway up the sides of the meat.

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:58.740
meat like that. Let that come to a boil. To that we're going to add

00:15:00.636 --> 00:15:02.636
A liberal tablespoon of tomato paste.

00:15:07.836 --> 00:15:08.856
out of my famous...

00:15:09.180 --> 00:15:10.420
Tooth-based tube here.

00:15:11.292 --> 00:15:12.592
A couple of bay leaves.

00:15:15.580 --> 00:15:16.440
A little more than a couple.

00:15:19.996 --> 00:15:21.996
and about a half a teaspoon of thyme.

00:15:22.844 --> 00:15:25.164
You want to be careful with spices and herbs.

00:15:25.564 --> 00:15:26.144
The, uh.

00:15:26.748 --> 00:15:28.088
Less is better than more.

00:15:31.600 --> 00:15:39.200
Now that's going to have to cook for probably a half an hour or longer to get to the right consistency.

00:15:39.400 --> 00:15:44.860
Now when you first start out, you want to put a cover on the pan and turn the heat down.

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:50.310
but at some point during the cooking, you'll want to take the cover off

00:15:50.310 --> 00:15:53.310
because you'll want to reduce the stock just a tiny bit, not a lot,

00:15:53.370 --> 00:15:57.360
because this is stock, so the flavor is already there.

00:15:57.460 --> 00:15:59.940
You're not trying to grab essence by reducing pan juice.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:07.090
or something, but you could reduce it a little. What I'm going to do now is swap burners here,

00:16:07.490 --> 00:16:14.340
because I need this burner here for the spatial. When that comes to a boil there, I'm going to

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:22.060
turn it down to a low heat and put the cover on it, I think. I know I am, because the cover is

00:16:22.060 --> 00:16:25.960
right in front of me. It's coming to a boil now.

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:40.070
turn that down to low and a half on number one put the cover on it now we've got a pot of boiling

00:16:40.070 --> 00:16:45.540
water here and we're going to make this spatial but before we make the spatial we're going to

00:16:45.540 --> 00:16:52.260
take a little break and we'll be back shortly and the reason we're off camera is that mr levy

00:16:52.260 --> 00:16:56.200
doesn't like to be recognized because if he's recognized then he can't be impartial in these

00:16:56.200 --> 00:16:59.860
restaurants. You talk about Jasper White and Lydia Shire.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:02.900
And I said, nobody's perfect.

00:17:03.040 --> 00:17:05.880
People say to me, your rating goes to five stars.

00:17:05.900 --> 00:17:07.700
How many restaurants have five stars?

00:17:07.840 --> 00:17:09.600
Tell me the five-star restaurants in Boston.

00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:11.160
And I say, there aren't any.

00:17:11.340 --> 00:17:13.340
And they say, how could there not be any?

00:17:13.460 --> 00:17:15.220
And I say, well, I'll tell you why.

00:17:15.880 --> 00:17:19.580
To become a five-star restaurant in my system,

00:17:19.580 --> 00:17:22.920
if I went there with you and two other people three times,

00:17:23.720 --> 00:17:27.270
that's 12 dinners, four people three times,

00:17:27.450 --> 00:17:29.980
to get five stars not only...

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:34.680
would the place have to be beautiful, very clean, very kempt, very interesting looking.

00:17:34.960 --> 00:17:39.760
Not only would all of the attention from the greeting at the door, to every bit of service,

00:17:39.920 --> 00:17:46.280
to every replacement of silver, to every refilling of the cup of water, to dessert service, not

00:17:46.280 --> 00:17:53.770
only would all of that have to be flawless, peerless, but twelve times this kitchen would

00:17:53.770 --> 00:17:59.630
have to put out four appetizers, four salads, four entrees, four desserts.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:05.320
different and all peerless well you can't do it you can't do it I mean it

00:18:05.320 --> 00:18:09.340
could happen I have twice in my six years on the five and a half years as a

00:18:09.340 --> 00:18:15.510
critic done short little pieces in my what my notes column that runs every

00:18:15.510 --> 00:18:20.370
other Tuesday called a la carte rather than my reviews which run every week I

00:18:20.370 --> 00:18:25.210
have done about half the column devoted to a dining experience I had that I

00:18:25.210 --> 00:18:29.910
regarded as a five-star experience but in each case it was me and

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:37.380
one other person two of us and one meal in a great restaurant but so perfection is very elusive

00:18:37.380 --> 00:18:44.160
and and sometimes I feel lousy when I've given a fairly high rating anything from three or up is

00:18:44.160 --> 00:18:49.110
now that I've been doing this all this time usually brings a great crush of business to

00:18:49.110 --> 00:18:54.260
these restaurants and if I give a restaurant a three and a half and then I for one reason or

00:18:54.260 --> 00:18:59.960
another. It falls off in quality with a change of chef or other reasons.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:05.340
reasons, it really, I feel heart-sick about it sometimes and I'm bothered by it.

00:19:05.440 --> 00:19:10.560
So even when I'm doing new restaurants that are open a few months, I try through my experience

00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:16.480
to get that sort of almost intuitive sense that this is the kind of place that not only

00:19:16.480 --> 00:19:21.420
is doing it well now, but probably would continue to do it well.

00:19:21.600 --> 00:19:26.840
And sometimes you get a feeling that you've gone to a restaurant and it was awful good,

00:19:26.840 --> 00:19:28.980
but you can't believe it, sort of.

00:19:30.160 --> 00:19:36.130
very frequently you go your other visits and it drops off and you know that you hit them on a

00:19:36.130 --> 00:19:41.960
especially good night and you give them credit for that but you don't give them a rating that

00:19:41.960 --> 00:19:47.760
would be the same as if you were only judging it by that night most people most people's dining

00:19:47.760 --> 00:19:52.060
experience let's face it is friday and saturday nights and then the worst nights in a restaurant

00:19:52.060 --> 00:19:57.200
and i have to tell you as you would guess i almost never go to a restaurant on saturday for reviewing

00:19:57.200 --> 00:19:59.320
purposes, just because I think

00:19:59.320 --> 00:19:59.980
Eva...

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:02.860
Again, it's forgiving them, giving them the benefit of the doubt.

00:20:02.940 --> 00:20:06.600
I think the night that they're most crowded and most pressured and most pressed,

